#version 450

/*
[QL] R13 step 3c: the ambient occlusion denoiser.

Four rays per pixel is not an estimate of occlusion, it is a coin flipped four
times. The mean is right and the variance is enormous, which on screen is a
field of grain that crawls when you move. The fix is not more rays - it is to
notice that the answer is nearly the same for neighbouring pixels *on the same
surface*, and to average the ones that are.

"On the same surface" is the whole difficulty, and it is why this is not a
Gaussian blur. A plain blur drags the dark of a corner out across the wall it
meets and smears occlusion onto the sky, because it weighs neighbours by
distance on screen, and two pixels one texel apart can be a metre apart in the
world. So each tap is weighted twice: by how far away it is on screen, and by
how much its depth disagrees with the centre's. A neighbour across a silhouette
contributes nothing.

Separable, in two passes. A 9x9 bilateral kernel is 81 taps; the same kernel as
a horizontal pass then a vertical one is 18. Bilateral filters are not strictly
separable - the range weight makes the two-pass result differ from the true 2D
one near an edge - but the difference is invisible on a term this smooth, and
the factor of four and a half is not.

The second pass is also the composite: it is built a second time with multiply
blending against the main framebuffer, so the vertical blur and the darkening of
the scene are one draw rather than two. That is why the shader writes plain AO
rather than anything pre-multiplied, and why the blend state is the only
difference between the two pipelines built from it.

USE_MSAA builds the variant that reads a multisampled depth attachment, for the
same reason rtao.tmpl has one: with r_ext_multisample on there is no
single-sample depth image to read.
*/

layout(set = 0, binding = 0) uniform sampler2D aoMap;
#ifdef USE_MSAA
layout(set = 0, binding = 1) uniform sampler2DMS depthMap;
#else
layout(set = 0, binding = 1) uniform sampler2D depthMap;
#endif

layout(location = 0) in vec2 frag_tex_coord;
layout(location = 0) out vec4 out_color;

layout(push_constant) uniform Push {
	/*
	xy  the step between taps, in texels, along the axis this pass blurs.
	    (1,0) horizontal, (0,1) vertical, (0,0) means "do not blur" - see
	    below, it is how r_rtaoDenoise 0 turns this into a passthrough without
	    needing a second pipeline.
	zw  unused.
	*/
	vec4 step;
	/*
	x,y  the projection terms that turn a depth value into a view-space
	     distance: dist = y / (depth + x), taken from elements 10 and 14 of the
	     projection matrix the scene was drawn with.

	     Comparing raw depth values instead does not work at all. Depth is
	     distributed hyperbolically, so the same raw difference is a
	     centimetre near the camera and fifty metres across the room; a
	     tolerance that keeps edges sharp up close blurs through walls at
	     distance, and one that holds at distance kills the filter in front of
	     you. Linearising first makes one tolerance correct everywhere.

	     This works unchanged under reversed depth: negating and offsetting
	     elements 10 and 14, which is what the reversed transform does, leaves
	     y/(d+x) algebraically identical.

	z    depth tolerance, as a fraction of the centre pixel's distance. A
	     neighbour further than this contributes nothing.
	w    unused.
	*/
	vec4 depthLinear;
} pc;

/*
Taps per side. A specialization constant so the loop is unrolled at the value in
use and there is no per-pixel branch on a number that changes once a session.
*/
layout(constant_id = 0) const int blurRadius = 4;

/*
One body for both variants: texelFetch's third argument is a sample index for
sampler2DMS and a mip level for sampler2D, and 0 is what this pass wants either
way. The #ifdef is on the declaration above, where the types genuinely differ -
sampler2D cannot read a multisampled image at all.
*/
float fetchDepth(ivec2 p) {
	return texelFetch(depthMap, p, 0).r;
}

float linearise(float d) {
	return pc.depthLinear.y / (d + pc.depthLinear.x);
}

void main() {
	ivec2 c = ivec2(gl_FragCoord.xy);

	/*
	r_rtaoDenoise 0. Returning the raw trace here rather than building a
	separate passthrough pipeline keeps one code path for the composite, which
	this pass also is - the alternative is a second pipeline that exists only to
	not do something.
	*/
	if (pc.step.x == 0.0 && pc.step.y == 0.0) {
		out_color = vec4(texelFetch(aoMap, c, 0).r);
		return;
	}

	ivec2 size = textureSize(aoMap, 0);
	ivec2 dir = ivec2(pc.step.xy);

	float centerDepth = linearise(fetchDepth(c));
	/*
	Absolute tolerance in world units, scaled by how far away the centre is. A
	fixed fraction of distance is the right shape: a wall seen from across the
	map occupies few pixels and its depth changes fast per pixel, so the window
	has to open as it recedes or the filter stops working exactly where the
	noise is most visible.

	The +1 keeps it from collapsing to zero at the near plane, where centreDepth
	is a few units and every tap would otherwise be rejected.
	*/
	float tolerance = pc.depthLinear.z * centerDepth + 1.0;

	float sum = texelFetch(aoMap, c, 0).r;
	float weightSum = 1.0;

	/*
	Gaussian falloff chosen so the outermost tap still carries about 10% of the
	centre's weight - wide enough that the kernel is not effectively smaller
	than it claims, narrow enough that it does not behave like a box filter.
	*/
	float sigma = max(float(blurRadius), 1.0) * 0.5;
	float spatialScale = -0.5 / (sigma * sigma);

	for (int i = 1; i <= blurRadius; i++) {
		float spatial = exp(float(i * i) * spatialScale);

		for (int s = -1; s <= 1; s += 2) {
			/*
			Clamped rather than skipped. texelFetch outside the image is
			undefined, not zero, and clamping repeats the edge texel - which
			for a term this smooth is the correct extrapolation anyway.
			*/
			ivec2 p = clamp(c + dir * (i * s), ivec2(0), size - ivec2(1));

			float d = linearise(fetchDepth(p));
			float range = max(0.0, 1.0 - abs(d - centerDepth) / tolerance);
			float w = spatial * range;

			sum += texelFetch(aoMap, p, 0).r * w;
			weightSum += w;
		}
	}

	out_color = vec4(sum / weightSum);
}
